# Skill Authoring

Per ADR-015 (mt#2251), skill authoring is **hybrid** and **location-canonical**.

## Where skills live

- **Canonical source:** `.minsky/skills/<name>/` — EITHER `skill.ts` (`defineSkill`, type-safe, schema-validated) OR a markdown source (`SKILL.md` / `content.md`). Canonicity is by **location**, not format. Pick TS for skills with structured metadata; markdown for prose-dominant skills.
- **Compile output:** `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` — ALWAYS generated by `compile --target claude-skills`; carries the `# Generated by minsky compile. Do not edit directly.` banner on line 2. Never hand-edit it — the `check-generated-file-edit` hook blocks direct edits.
- **Vendored community skills (exception):** a skill committed under `.claude/skills/<name>/` with a `_VENDORED.md` marker (or installed via the `skills` CLI / skills.sh into `.agents/skills/`) is **upstream-canonical**. Do NOT author a `.minsky/` source for it; update it by refetching from the upstream source URL in `_VENDORED.md`. Unified vendored handling is owned by mt#1908.

## Canonical workflow

1. Edit the canonical source under `.minsky/skills/<name>/` (the `.ts` or the markdown).
2. Run `bun src/cli.ts compile --target claude-skills` to regenerate `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`.
3. Commit BOTH the source and the regenerated output together. The pre-commit staleness check (`runCompileCheck` in `src/hooks/pre-commit.ts`) blocks commits where the output is stale relative to its source.

## Anti-patterns

- **Editing `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` directly.** It is a compile output; the edit is reverted on the next recompile, and the generated-file-edit guard blocks it. Edit the `.minsky/` source instead.
- **Authoring a new skill only in `.claude/skills/`.** That is the harness-output directory, not an authoring directory. New authored skills go under `.minsky/skills/<name>/`.
- **Creating a `.minsky/` source for a vendored skill.** Vendored skills are upstream-canonical; a local source falsely claims authorship and breaks refetch-update.

## Cross-reference convention

When a skill references a SIBLING skill, use the harness-neutral slash form `/<skill-name>` (e.g. `/restate-plan`) — NOT a path into the harness-output dir (`.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`) or the source dir (`.minsky/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`). The slash form is correct in BOTH the `.minsky/` source and the compiled `.claude/` output (the same body serves both), and it matches the dominant existing convention. Decided in mt#2302 under ADR-015 location-canonicity.

When a sibling reference needs to be a clickable link (e.g. pointing at a specific section), keep a **relative** target and put the slash form in the display label: `` [`/minsky-brand`](../minsky-brand/SKILL.md) §6 ``. The relative `../<name>/SKILL.md` target is harness-neutral (it resolves correctly in both the `.minsky/` source tree and the compiled `.claude/` tree); only the display label must be the `/<skill-name>` slash form.

**Exception — descriptive directory mentions are not cross-references.** Stating where outputs live or where the harness discovers skills (e.g. "all skill files under `.claude/skills/`", "auto-discovered from `.claude/skills/`") is a factual description, not a sibling link; leave the literal path.

## Description budget: a description is a routing key, not documentation (mt#3476)

**Target ≤ 250 chars, hard cap 400**, measured on the compiled `description`. A description's
only job is to let the router decide whether to load the skill; triggers, methodology, and
cross-references belong in the BODY, which loads on invocation and costs nothing until then.

**Why the cap binds on OTHER skills, not just yours.** The injected available-skills listing is
charged against a shared CUMULATIVE budget: entries are emitted until it is spent, and each one
lands only if it still fits. An over-long description does not merely cost itself — it consumes
budget that later entries needed, and they lose their descriptions entirely. A skill listed
name-only cannot be routed to, so it is installed, enabled, and invisible.

That is not hypothetical: on 2026-07-31, thirteen skills reached a live session with no
description while every one of them carried a full `description` on disk. The mechanism (a
greedy fill against a budget in the [5708, 5853)-char range) is documented in mt#3487 — it is a
model fit against observed output, not a read of the harness, so treat the exact figure as an
estimate and the discipline as the point.

The `claude-skills` compile target enforces this: every compile reports the listing total
and names any over-cap description. Check it directly while working on descriptions:

```bash
bun run skills:budget         # report: per-description lengths + listing total
bun run skills:budget:check   # non-zero exit when over cap or over total
```

**Vendored skills are exempt from the cap, not from the cost.** Their description is set
upstream and a local edit is reverted on the next refetch (ADR-015), so `--check` reports them
and does not fail on them — but they still consume the shared budget. Trimming an OWNED
description is the only lever that frees budget for them.

